label rumors
[signal arg="bg:cafeteria_morning"]
Enriko: Three different people told me you used a forbidden technique. One version has you channeling gi through your teeth.
Enriko: I don't even know what that would accomplish. Another says Han's family is going to formally protest to the Headmaster.
Enriko: A third says you're secretly the bastard son of a war hero, which is the most boring theory circulating, and I'm disappointed in whoever started it.
Lan (Default): None of that happened.
Enriko: Obviously. Doesn't matter. By dinner there'll be six more versions.
Enriko: You're not rank {rank} anymore, if that wasn't clear.
Enriko: You're rank {rank}, allegedly, according to a piece of paper nobody currently believes.
label amir_nod
[signal arg="bg:training_yard"]
He was crossing the training hall's outer yard when he saw Instructor Amir, standing exactly where memory now insisted he'd stood during the duel.
Arms crossed, weight settled, watching Lan cross the yard with an expression that gave away nothing.
Amir didn't call him over. When Lan met his eyes, Amir gave the smallest possible nod. Not approval, not disapproval. Acknowledgment.
Lan (Sus): (Berthram. Leonora. Ilia. Liesel, in her own way. And now him. A list I can no longer pretend is short.)
label raiz
He almost walked past the man leaning against the yard wall. White hair, desert gray, a straight-backed, coiled kind of stillness.
Raiz: Schwert.
Lan (Sus): (Raiz Al Khalid. Rank one.)
Raiz: You beat Han with a practice sword. He was taking something. I don't care about the something.
Raiz: I care about the four minutes before it, where you were giving ground on purpose. Why?
- Answer directly
	set {aff_raiz} += 2
	Lan (Default): To read his rhythm. His rapier work is textbook and his feet are lazy.
	Lan (Default): By the fourth exchange I knew where he'd overcommit. The pill just made him faster. It didn't fix any of that.
	Raiz: Hm. That's a real answer. Most people would say they were lucky.
	Raiz: Han's guard is trash. You're the first who bothered saying why.
- Play it down
	set {aff_raiz} += 0
	Lan (Acting): I panicked and it went well.
	Raiz: I've heard that line from cowards and liars. Which are you?
	Lan (Default): Neither. Just tired.
	Raiz: Hm.
- Make a joke
	set {aff_raiz} -= 1
	Lan (Smug): Practice sword, practice guard. I was getting my steps in.
	Raiz: I don't have patience for jokes. Come back when you have an argument.
Raiz: Al-Tale'a measures people by what they do, not their number. I'll be watching, Schwert.
label kliam
[signal arg="bg:library_corner"]
Lan (Talk): Have you ever heard the name Kliam?
Enriko's easy expression flickered. Not recognition exactly. The alertness of a man realizing a question had weight behind it.
Enriko: Should I have?
Lan (Default): There was a pill. Han had it on him during the fight. I want to know where something like that comes from.
Enriko: An unmarked pill at a school full of teenagers who'd kill for an edge on the rank list.
Enriko: That's the kind of thing that gets an academy shut down if it's traced back far enough.
Lan (Talk): Can you find out anything?
Enriko: I can ask around. Carefully. My family does business in the lower district, officially and otherwise.
Enriko: But asking loudly is a good way to get hurt by people who aren't first-years with something to prove.
Lan (Default): I'm aware.
Enriko: I know you're aware.
Enriko: I'm saying it anyway, because you have a bad habit of hearing a warning and doing the thing regardless.
Enriko: Try not to duel anyone else in the meantime. My toast budget can't take the stress.
- Ask how Veldero is holding up
	Lan (Talk): How's Veldero doing? He's been quiet.
	Enriko: He's fine. He reads the syllabus now. It's terrifying.
- Move on
	Lan (Smug): I'll try. No promises.
set {aff_enriko} += 1
label night
[signal arg="bg:east_wing_night"]
The east wing was quiet and cold, the moonlight cutting its usual long silver stripe.
Lan had lost three exchanges in a row before he'd registered that he was losing.
Dialog/Ilia (Def): You're somewhere else tonight. I should warn you I don't slow down for off nights. That's not what you're paying me for.
Lan (Default): I'm listening.
Dialog/Ilia (Def): You're really not.
[signal arg="combat:spar_ilia_distracted"]
Lan (Sus): (Kliam. A mid-tier threat, buried deep, not supposed to surface for real until the second year.)
Lan (Sus): (If he's already moving, what else is moving that I don't have a page for? If he shows up before I'm ready, I'd lose.)
Lan (Sus): (Not struggle. Lose.)
He didn't see the fifth attack coming at all.
She swept his legs, clean and precise, and put him on his back with her rapier resting lightly against his throat.
Dialog/Ilia (Def): That's a real hit, from someone who was actually trying, against someone who very clearly wasn't. Which is new.
Dialog/Ilia (Def): You don't usually let me get that close.
Liesel (Flat): You've been like this for two days. Since the duel. You go somewhere else in the middle of conversations.
Liesel (Flat): You didn't finish your food twice this week, and you never leave food unfinished.
Liesel (Flat): What happened out there that you haven't told anyone?
Dialog/Ilia (Def): She's right. I've sparred with a lot of distracted people. This isn't distracted. This is carrying something.
- Give them the largest true piece
	set {aff_liesel} += 1
	set {aff_ilia} += 1
	Lan (Sus): There's something coming. Bigger than Han and his pill. I don't know exactly when, or from where.
- Say it plainly
	set {aff_liesel} += 1
	Lan (Default): Something's coming. Bigger than the pill. I don't have more than that yet.
Lan (Sus): I just know it's early, earlier than it should be, and I'm not ready for it yet.
Liesel (Default): Then we get you ready. That's not a question. That's just what happens now.
Dialog/Ilia (Happy): Whatever's coming early, it's going to have to get through the three of us first. Personally, I like those odds.
label ledger
[signal arg="bg:records_office"]
In the same records office that had become more her office than a borrowed one,
Leonora von Rosendael added a third name to a short list written in her own careful hand, crossed out a fourth who had an alibi, and sat back.
Two names. A forty-minute gap in a patrol schedule.
And a growing certainty that whoever had authorized it was someone whose name she'd recognize the moment she proved it.
She did not sleep well that night. She kept working anyway.
